# Example 9: Central dogma pipeline - coupled transcription-translation
# Shows protein concentration accumulating per tick under GRN regulation of multiple genes
#
# p_housekeeping constitutive expression (strength<0 means always active)
#   -> rnap (RNA polymerase) synthesis -> activates p_target
#   -> ribosome synthesis -> increases translation efficiency
#   -> target_protein concentration accumulates each tick
#
# This .helix file explicitly declares central dogma steps via the #transcribe / #translate bio-instruction annotations,
# and enables use_central_dogma=true in #config so the VM performs transcription-translation in the tick loop;
# the #type annotation marks target_protein as a Protein type.
# True "coupled transcription-translation" (where the ribosome follows the RNA polymerase in prokaryotes) is described via the Python API below:

#promoter name=p_housekeeping strength=-0.5
#promoter name=p_target strength=0.4

#gene name=rnap promoter=p_housekeeping
ATG GCT GCT GCT TAA
#end

#gene name=ribosome promoter=p_housekeeping
ATG GGT GGT GGT TAA
#end

#gene name=target_protein promoter=p_target
ATG GCT GGT GCT GGT TAA
#end

#regulate p_housekeeping -> rnap strength=+0.7
#regulate p_housekeeping -> ribosome strength=+0.7
#regulate rnap -> p_target strength=+0.6
#regulate ribosome -> p_target strength=+0.5
#regulate p_target -> target_protein strength=+0.9

# Central dogma instructions: explicitly declare transcription of rnap and translation of ribosome
# The parser records them as BioInstructions (kind=transcribe / translate, target=...)
#transcribe target=rnap
#translate target=ribosome

# Type annotation (P0-1.3): marks target_protein as Protein
# The parser records it in prog.type_annotations = {"target_protein": "Protein"}
#type target_protein=Protein

# Note: the following is a Python API example for coupled transcription-translation (equivalent to the #transcribe/#translate annotations above)
# --- for use in scripts (not .helix annotations) ---
#
